shape theory.structure : theory.shape {
  type: definition
  layer: 0
  """
// Definition 3.1 (Structure).
// The structure of a shape, denoted S, is the aspect that stays the
// same and governs the transformation: the geometry of how the shape
// persists. Structure defines what kind of shape this is.
//
// Forced by the axiom: persistence requires both change and continuity.
// By excluded middle, every aspect either changes or does not. What
// does not change is structure. What changes is character.
// Exactly two categories, exhaustive and mutually exclusive.
//
// Structure is not passively invariant. It must govern the transformation.
// If it did not, any change could produce any result, and there would
// be no continuity between one state and the next. The invariant aspect
// must determine how change happens. This is what makes it structure:
// it is load-bearing. It carries the transformation.
//
// Structure is constant across ordinary transformations. It can change
// through coherence resolution with other shapes (structural change,
// derived in theory.algebra.structural-change).
//
// Derives from: theory.shape (the axiom forces the S/C decomposition)
  """
}
